WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:10.782
SuiFleet helps you deliver software to devices and see what
happened. Start with one machine, then organize a fleet into
groups. Here is the idea behind the controls.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:25.035
Your wallet is the app you use to approve management changes. It
signs your request so the network can check your authority. Each
device has its own identity. Your wallet's private key is not
sent to the devices.

3
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:43.914
Publishing puts a version and its verification details in shared
Sui records. The software files live in Walrus storage for a
chosen period. Confidential releases are encrypted, with Seal
controlling who can obtain the keys to open them. Publishing
alone installs nothing.

4
00:00:45.150 --> 00:01:00.446
You approve a deployment for a device or group. The shared
records describe the approved version and targets. Each device
client checks those records on its configured schedule when
connected. This is not a remote session pushing files into your
machines.

5
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:20.061
A targeted device downloads the files, verifies the approved
release and runs its configured installer. Your platform handles
system update details, including any reboot or rollback. An
offline device can catch up later, while the deployment permits
it and the files remain available.

6
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:38.879
The device authenticates its result so the console can show
whether installation succeeded or failed. Health is reported
separately. A deployment request does not mean installed, and
installed does not mean healthy. Reporting frequency determines
how fresh that picture is.

7
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:56.079
The console, shared records, file storage and device clients
work together without a SuiFleet application server. Network
providers, connectivity and operating funds are still needed.
Try one device first, then use groups and staged rollouts to
expand.

